      "title": "Assistant Professor - Special Education",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Special Education | NEIU Skip to main content Join us at NEIU's Graduate Open House on Oct. 20. Learn more . Admissions Learn About NEIU Visit Us Our Programs Requirements Admitted Students Your Success Profile Graduate Studies Continue Your Education Parents and Family Resources One Stop Center Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Tuition Information Fees Information Calculating Your Net Cost Scholarship Opportunities Scholarship Listing Applying for Federal & State Assistance Documents Summer Financial Aid Resources Financial Aid Forms & Documents Resources Applying for Student Loans Student Employment Opportunities Student Payment Services Academics Taking Classes Class Schedules & Registration Academic Calendar Advising Center Majors, Minors, Graduate Programs & Certificates University Core Curriculum / General Education Community & Professional Education Academic Units All Academic Departments College of Arts & Sciences College of Business & Technology Daniel L. Goodwin College of Education College of Graduate Studies & Research Nontraditional Degree Programs Carruthers Center for Inner City Studies El Centro University Center of Lake County Center for College Access and Success First-Year Experience Our Centers and Programs Opportunities International Programs Research Resources McNair Scholars University Honors Program Student Center for Science Engagement Intensive English Program Resources Center for Teaching and Learning Registrar Services Transcripts Academic Catalog Student Achievement Information Assessment University Life Things To Do Academic Calendar Campus Recreation Student Union Arts at Northeastern Box Office Student Leadership & Organizations Support Learning Success Center TRIO Student Support Services Project Success Proyecto Pa’Lante Student Affairs Dean of Students Advising Center Student Success and Retention Services All Student Resources Student Counseling Services Career Development Student Health Services Student Disability Services Veterans Services Parking University Police Bookstore Child Care Center Technology Services Event & Conference Services Immigration Enforcement Actions Resources Library Pedroso Center Undocumented Student Resources Black Student Success Committee Title IX New Student Resources Guide BIG Center Living on Campus Student Housing Living Learning Communities Alumni & Giving Alumni Your Membership Alumni Events Office of Alumni Relations Giving Giving Opportunities Impact of Your Gift Scholarships Foundation Meet Our Staff Transforming Lives About Who We Are Northeastern News Facts & Distinctions Community & Visitor Guide Northeastern History Mission, Vision and Values Strategic Planning Accreditation Office of Institutional Research & Assessment Our Leadership University Leadership President Executive Team Shared Governance Board of Trustees Employment Working Here Resources Brand Guide Complaints Northeastern Neighbors Legal Menu Audience Menu Audience Placeholder What can we help you find? Menu Actions Menu Actions Give Now Request Info Visit Apply Home — \n                                Assistant Professor - Special Education Assistant Professor - Special Education Anticipated Starting Date August 2027 Rank Assistant Professor (tenure track) Salary Range $65,000 - $68.000 View Employee Benefits Position The Programs in Special Education within the Department of Literacy, Leadership, Development and Special Education at Northeastern Illinois University invite applications for a full-time, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Special Education , beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will join a collaborative faculty committed to preparing highly skilled, reflective, and equity-minded special education professionals to serve students with disabilities and their families in diverse schools and communities. The Programs in Special Education offer undergraduate, graduate, licensure, and endorsement pathways, including the Bachelor of Arts in Special Education (LBS I), Master of Arts in Special Education (LBS I), and Master of Science in Special Education (LBS II). The successful candidate should possess broad expertise in special education and the ability to contribute across undergraduate and graduate programs. Of particular interest are candidates whose professional experiences include special education administration, educational leadership, program development, educator preparation, or systems-level improvement in special education. About Northeastern Illinois University Founded in 1867, Northeastern Illinois University (NEIU) enrolls about 5,700 students and is regarded as one of the most diverse public comprehensive universities in the Midwest. The Main Campus is located on 67 acres in a residential area on the Northwest Side of Chicago and two additional campuses are located in the Avondale and Bronzeville neighborhoods of Chicago. Since 1997, the U.S. Department of Education has designated NEIU a Hispanic-Serving Institution. NEIU offers more than 40 undergraduate degree and certificate programs and more than 50 graduate degree, certificate, licensure and endorsement programs. Read more about Northeastern Illinois University at www.neiu.edu . The Goodwin College of Education (GCOE) enrolls approximately 1,200 students in nine undergraduate and twenty-one graduate degree programs spanning the areas of counselor education, educational leadership, teacher education, and community and health studies. With roots that trace back to the first teacher education institution in Chicago, the college today is one of the largest producers of diverse educators in Illinois and the Chicago area. Educator licensure programs are accredited by the Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP) and counseling programs have specialized accreditation through the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP). Read more about the college at www.neiu.edu/gcoe . Programs in Special Education Northeastern Illinois University offers a comprehensive continuum of preparation in special education through its Bachelor of Arts and Master of Arts in Special Education (LBS I) programs . Together, these programs prepare candidates for initial Illinois teacher licensure and for effective practice with school-age students with a wide range of exceptional learning needs, with emphasis on assessment, instructional planning, evidence-based instruction, technology, and inclusive educational practice. The Master of Science in Special Education (LBS II) provides advanced preparation for experienced special educators seeking specialist and leadership expertise. Graduate candidates may pursue preparation in Curriculum Adaptation Specialist (CAS), Behavior Intervention Specialist (BIS), or both areas. Faculty within the Programs in Special Education contribute across these programs and their campus locations as well as endorsement, approval, clinical, and professional-development initiatives. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses (all campus locations and instructional modalities - online, hybrid, and in-person) across the Programs in Special Education, including courses within the B.A. in Special Education, M.A. in Special Education (LBS I), and M.S. in Special Education (LBS II) programs according to program needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. Teach courses related to the characteristics and education of students with disabilities, assessment, evidence-based instructional practices, behavioral supports, curriculum adaptation, collaboration, inclusive practices, special education policy, leadership, and other areas represented within the Special Education curriculum. Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to a tenure-track faculty appointment, resulting in peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and other scholarly or professional contributions. Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate candidates and support candidates as they progress through academic, clinical, licensure, and professional requirements. Participate in curriculum development, program assessment, and continuous program improvement. Contribute to program, department, college, university, and professional service. Collaborate with faculty, school districts, community partners, and other stakeholders to strengthen educator preparation and professional learning opportunities. Participate in the development, implementation, and evaluation of clinical and field-based experiences in special education. Support educator preparation accreditation, state approval, licensure, and program review activities. Contribute to recruitment, retention, and support initiatives for undergraduate and graduate candidates. Pursue opportunities for external funding, grants, partnerships, and other initiatives that advance the mission and priorities of the Programs in Special Education and the Daniel L. Goodwin College of Education. Participate actively in the shared governance and collaborative work of the Programs in Special Education and the Department of Literacy, Leadership, Development and Special Education. Required Qualifications Applicants must meet the following qualifications: Earned Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Special Education or a closely related field, such as Educational Psychology, from an accredited institution; ABD candidates will be considered provided all requirements for the doctoral degree will be completed and the degree awarded no later than August 1, 2027. Graduate preparation, scholarship, and/or professional experience demonstrating substantive expertise in special education. Demonstrated knowledge of contemporary evidence-based practices for educating students with disabilities in K–12 settings. K–12 teaching experience in special education, preferably as a licensed special education teacher. Ability to teach a broad range of undergraduate and/or graduate courses within the Programs in Special Education Demonstrated commitment to effective teaching, scholarship, and service within a diverse urban university environment. Evidence of the ability to work effectively and collaboratively with candidates, faculty, school professionals, families, and community partners from diverse backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates demonstrating one or more of the following: Professional credentials, licensure, endorsements, or substantial professional experience in special education administration or educational leadership with an explicit focus on special education programs and services. Experience serving students with disabilities across multiple instructional environments, grade levels, disability categories, or service-delivery models. Experience developing or participating in externally funded grants or other sponsored projects in higher education or educator preparation. Experience with accreditation, state program approval, licensure review, or continuous-improvement processes, particularly those involving CAEP, the Illinois State Board of Education, the Council for Exceptional Children standards, or comparable professional and governmental bodies. Higher education teaching experience, particularly in undergraduate or graduate educator preparation programs. Experience supervising, mentoring, coaching, or evaluating special education teachers, related-service professionals, or other school personnel. Experience with special education program administration, compliance, instructional leadership, systems-level implementation, or improvement initiatives within schools or school districts. Expertise related to advanced special education practice, including curriculum adaptation, behavioral intervention, assessment, inclusive education, instructional technology, or collaboration. Experience teaching or mentoring graduate candidates preparing for advanced leadership or specialist roles in special education. Evidence of successful scholarly activity, including peer-reviewed publications and professional conference presentations. Experience using candidate, program, or outcome data for accreditation reporting, program evaluation, and continuous improvement. Experience developing or revising university curricula, academic programs, endorsements, certificates, or educator preparation pathways. Experience establishing partnerships among universities, schools, districts, professional organizations, or community agencies. Demonstrated potential for leadership within university-based special education programs. Deadline Screening will begin immediately and will end when the position is filled. The successful candidate must provide documentation verifying completion of the doctoral degree no later than August 1, 2027 . Additional materials may be requested from candidates advancing in the search process. Apply via Workday https://neiu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/NEIU Applicants should submit: A letter of application addressing the qualifications and responsibilities of the position; Curriculum vitae; Statement of teaching philosophy and interests; Statement of research/scholarly agenda; Evidence of teaching effectiveness, if available; Graduate transcripts; and Contact information for three professional references. Northeastern Illinois University welcomes candidates who will support our diverse and inclusive student community. Northeastern is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer and invites applications from Women, Minorities, Veterans and Persons with Disabilities, as well as other Qualified Individuals. Northeastern Illinois University’s positions are contingent upon the University's receipt of its State of Illinois appropriation. View Printer Friendly Our Mission is You Request Information Plan a Visit Apply Now Footer Menu 1 Footer Menu 1 Online Learning Academic Calendar NEIUPort Maps & Directions Housing Events Employment Directory Community Library Main Campus 5500 North St. Louis Avenue Chicago, IL 60625 (773) 583-4050 Carruthers Center 700 East Oakwood Blvd. Chicago, IL 60625 (773) 268-7500 El Centro 3390 North Avondale Ave. Chicago, IL 60618 (773) 442-4080 University Center 1200 University Center Drive, Room 220B Grayslake, IL 60030 (847) 665-4000 Footer bottom Footer bottom Take a Virtual Tour Give to NEIU © 2022 Northeastern Illinois University | Legal | Login Also of Interest High-Quality Access-Driven Online Credit Courses Student Services and Support in Illinois University Campus Virtual Tour ↑"
    }
